== 8.1 Leisure and Recreation

*Leisure* generally refers to time free from required work or other obligations. *Recreation* refers to activities people choose for enjoyment, relaxation or restoration. A recreational activity can be physical, such as walking or dancing, or relatively quiet, such as reading or a board game. The same activity may be experienced differently by different people.

Recreation can support enjoyment, social connection and opportunities for movement. Its effects depend on the person, activity and context; it should not be described as a guaranteed treatment for a health condition. Access may be affected by cost, transport, safety, available facilities and other circumstances.

#rule-box("Time and activity")[
  Leisure is the available free time; recreation is an activity chosen during that time for enjoyment or restoration. A person can use leisure in different ways.
]

== 8.2 Dance and Movement

Dance combines movement and rhythm and can have social, cultural, recreational or performance purposes. A dance activity may involve individual or group participation and can develop coordination or expression. Traditions and styles vary; respectful learning includes recognising their cultural context rather than treating all dances as identical.

As with other activity, consider the space, footwear, intensity and participant's needs. A person should not be required to continue through an injury or concerning symptoms. Preparation and appropriate rest can support participation without guaranteeing that every risk is removed.

== 8.3 Adapted Physical Activity

*Adapted physical activity* modifies an activity or its setting to support meaningful participation by people with differing needs and abilities. A teacher or coach may adjust equipment, rules, communication, space or pace. The appropriate modification depends on the person, the objective, available resources and safety considerations.

Ask the participant about their preferences and needs; do not assume that a diagnosis or disability label describes every ability. An adaptation can preserve the objective of a game while changing how it is performed. For example, a larger or differently textured ball may be useful for a particular learner, but it is not automatically the right choice for everyone.

